  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nLight rain chances (20 to 50%) return tonight into Thursday \nmorning: Cloud cover will increase this evening as weak \noverrunning moisture moves in ahead of a shortwave moving east \nacross southern Canada. Rain chances tonight generally range from \n20 to 50 percent, but precipitation will be light with 90th \npercentile amounts only up to 0.10 inches. Spotty light showers or\ndrizzle will linger into Thursday morning before clearing \ndevelops Thursday afternoon. Seasonable high temperatures ranging \nfrom the upper 60s to middle 70s are expected today and Thursday. \n\nHeavy rainfall potential Friday through Saturday: Heavy rainfall\nlate Friday into Saturday represents the most significant weather\nhazard during the 7-day forecast period, with increasing\npotential for localized flooding across central and east-central\nWisconsin. Shortwave energy advancing across the Rockies will\ntrigger a 30 to 40 knot low-level jet directed into central\nWisconsin Friday afternoon into Friday night. Strong dynamic\nforcing will overspread the region, characterized by upper-level\ndivergence in the right rear quadrant of an upper jet streak,\nrobust mid-level frontogenesis, and strong moisture convergence\nabove the returning warm front. Precipitable water values are\nforecast to climb to around 1.70 inches, which sits near the 96th\npercentile for mid- September. Warm cloud depths in excess of\n12,000 feet will favor efficient rainfall processes. \n\nModels continue to struggle with where the heaviest axis of heavy\nrain will occur. There are some signs that an initial band across\nthe far north on Friday afternoon associated with 700mb\nfrontogenesis would subsequently push the second band on Friday \nnight further south away from our area. However, there are still \nsigns the main band of heavy rain would drive through the heart of\nour area. This makes predicting rainfall amounts an issue as the \nNBM still seems too high with a mean value around 3 inches. The \nprevious forecast amounts of 1 to 3 inches along with the \nprobabilities still seem reasonable with the caveat that these \nfeatures need to be resolved before higher confidence can be \nassigned to where the swath of heavy rain will end up. If the same\nlocations that received heavy rainfall earlier in the week get \nthe swath of heavy rainfall, antecedent soil moisture remains \nhigh, elevating the threat of localized urban and small stream \nflooding. Severe weather risk remains low due to meager \ninstability, with most unstable capes generally under 500 J/kg.\n\nAdditional rain chances early next week: Following the departure\nof the aforementioned system, a brief break in precipitation is \nexpected Sunday as weak surface ridging passes over the Great \nLakes. However, the progressive mid-level flow will keep the \nregion in an unsettled regime heading into early next week. \nAnother upper shortwave trough and associated surface system will \napproach from the Great Plains toward Sunday night and Monday, \nbringing additional chances for rain showers and scattered \nthunderstorms through Tuesday.",
